\begin{csabstract}
In-play tennis prediction markets are priced by whoever learns the point first. We measure who that is, what they earn
and what each second is worth, from public trade tapes of << V('univ.matches') >> Polymarket tennis matches. A walk-forward fast tier of wallets trading within 3\,s of a score move earns
<< V('ft.c.is') >>¢ per share after fees in sample and << V('ft.c.oos') >>¢ in its single out-of-sample run; other
takers lose. At its own fills a frozen book earns << V('v2.oos.c') >>¢ on a burned (non-blind) hold-out, but not with
fees doubled. Simulated at a 1\,s licensed-feed baseline (<< CV_LABEL >>), a computer-vision (CV) trader earns
<< V('cv.pre.oos.usd') >> a day on that hold-out at the pre-registered stamp lag, where each second of feed delay costs
<< V('cv.pre.persec.range') >> a day, and << V('cv.cal.oos.usd') >> at a post hoc estimate, on trades selected on
outcomes; a replay on real books loses. Real money: none.

\vspace{2pt}\noindent\textbf{JEL:} G14, G12, C58.\enspace \textbf{Keywords:} prediction markets; latency arbitrage;
in-play betting; tennis; computer vision; market microstructure.
\end{csabstract}

